WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:22.222
Today, we are going to continue
thinking about reactions.

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:27.160
Just to remind you, what
did we do on Friday?

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.864
So we introduced-- here's
what we did last time.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:33.833
We talked about constant--
we talked about reactions.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:38.805
And there, on Friday, we were
really interested in rates--

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:40.206
how fast is it happening.

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:43.977
So we talked about things that
affect the reaction rate--

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:45.178
concentration.

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:49.949
Concentration versus time,
and how you can measure that.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:56.322
And you can have rate loss based
on experimental measurements.

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:59.459
And that can tell you
the reaction order.

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:02.429
We talked about zero, first,
second-order reactions,

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:04.664
the rate constant,
and then we also

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.801
talked about the
temperature dependence.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:10.603
And at the very end, the role
of a catalyst in lowering

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.506
that activation barrier.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:17.277
Today, we're going to talk
about how things dissolve.

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:22.949
So we're going to
talk about solubility,

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:26.453
and this has to do with
reactions because something

00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:29.122
dissolving is a reaction.

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.524
But what's going
to happen is we're

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:35.829
going to talk about how that
reaction finds its happy place.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:38.565
And we know a happy
place is a good thing,

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:41.768
and it's called equilibrium.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.902
And so that's going to
involve talking about things

00:01:43.902 --> 00:01:48.475
like equilibrium
constants, dissolution,

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:50.743
we're going to look
at some examples

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.512
with ionic compounds-- so salts.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:55.381
We're going to be
dissolving salts in water

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:59.252
and looking at how to think
about how much of that

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:01.955
dissolves in terms
of the equilibrium

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:03.890
constant for the reaction.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.326
So that's the goal today.

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:10.263
I want to start, though, by
talking about why this matters,

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:14.667
and that's directly
related to your goodie bag.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:19.939
So this is something some
of you may have seen.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.475
The data here in
from the '50s is

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:27.881
from the Mauna Loa measurements,
the top of a volcano.

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:31.718
And this is CO2 concentration,
parts per million

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:32.986
in the atmosphere.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:36.256
But if you dig into the ice--

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:37.891
we're going to be
doing a lot of that

00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:41.194
here in Boston over
the next three months.

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:45.531
But if you dig deeper into
the ice, in the cores,

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:49.402
you go back in time, and
it's a beautiful thing.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:53.973
The ice as you go down
is ancient atmosphere.

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.075
That's cool-- because
there's little bubbles.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:59.679
Remember, we talked
about the half life

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:06.152
of carbon-14 that's trapped from
a living thing breathing it in.

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:08.855
Well, you can trap
directly the little bubbles

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:12.025
of atmosphere in the ice,
and that's what happens.

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:15.328
And that's why we know what
CO2 levels-- we're going back

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:16.629
almost a million years here.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:19.232
It's 800,000 years of data.

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:23.570
And this is a graph that I
think many of you have seen.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.073
It's going up, a lot.

00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:27.974
And here it is.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:31.511
Now, this is just
zooming in on the last 50

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:36.049
or so-- this is just the
data, the Mauna Loa data.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.084
And The reason is
because here we also

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.352
have another kind of data.

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:42.222
So there's a lot of
attention paid on CO2,

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:44.224
and there should be.

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:48.328
There is a lot less
attention paid to what

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:51.731
that is doing to our oceans.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.334
And this is a reaction that
we will talk about more when

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:55.735
we talk about acidification.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:57.337
So when we talk about
acids and bases,

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:59.405
which is coming up later--

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:02.242
later being Wednesday
and next week--

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:03.376
we'll talk more about this.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.345
But I just want to
tell you that when

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:08.548
you have CO2 in the
atmosphere going up,

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:12.852
then you also have CO2
in the ocean going up.

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:14.587
So the CO2 in the ocean--

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:20.959
the CO2 in the atmosphere is
getting absorbed by the ocean.

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:22.328
So what?

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:26.499
No, it's a lot of what,
because what happens

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:28.835
is the CO2 in the ocean--

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:31.137
the more CO2 in the ocean--

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:35.207
is making the ocean more acidic.

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:37.510
So if you just write
this number down--

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.479
because it's so astonishing.

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:58.064
Past 200 years, the ocean
has become 30% more acidic.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:15.448
Now that is the fastest-known
change in the ocean chemistry

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:19.419
in 50 million years.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:30.830
That's a big and
very sudden change.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:36.369
So the ocean is absorbing about
22 million tons per day of CO2.

00:05:36.369 --> 00:05:37.970
And that number is going up.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.506
Why does that matter?

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:41.974
Maybe it will do
to the oceans what

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.644
it's doing to the atmosphere and
making things a little warmer.

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:49.215
No, it's doing more,
because when it acidifies,

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:51.183
then it has a direct
impact on things

00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:56.189
like this, which is a pteropod.

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:59.058
You look at the food
chain of the ocean-- there

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.793
are three things at the bottom.

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:03.429
That's one of them.

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:04.864
I mean, there's plants.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.799
There's photosynthesis.

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:09.502
And then there's the
bottom of the food chain.

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:11.371
Now you know what's
going to happen

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:15.541
when the bottom of the
food chain disappears.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.678
This is one of the
main kinds of animals

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:20.046
that lives at the
bottom of ocean

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:24.384
because they've got these very
small calcium carbonate shells.

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:27.954
If you make the
ocean just a little

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:33.393
more acidic, like we're doing,
those shells don't survive,

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:37.029
and those animals don't survive.

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:40.266
So that's what
happens to a pteropod.

00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:42.835
At the rate we're going--

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:47.473
like decades of time from
now-- that's what happens.

00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:50.643
So what you have
in your goodie bag

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:55.014
is a way to see this a
little bit more accelerated.

00:06:55.014 --> 00:06:56.349
So here's your goodie bag.

00:06:56.349 --> 00:06:58.217
So I wanted you to--

00:06:58.217 --> 00:07:01.354
I wanted you to see what's
happening in the ocean,

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:03.289
and you have now the
tools to do that.

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:07.593
So in your goodie bag,
you've got citric powder.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:10.062
Imagine you take a lot of
limes, and you squeeze them,

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:11.297
and then you dry it all out.

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:12.598
And that's what you got--

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.400
citric acid.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.104
Now you're going to mix that
with water to change the ph.

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:20.907
And you're going to
dissolve this powder.

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:23.709
That's the topic of today's
lecture-- dissolution

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:25.445
and equilibrium.

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:27.079
So you're going to
mix that into water,

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:32.018
and you've got a scale
from a previous goodie bag.

00:07:32.018 --> 00:07:34.821
So my hope is that you've been
using that on a daily basis.

00:07:34.821 --> 00:07:37.824
You take it with you places,
and you can use that again here.

00:07:37.824 --> 00:07:40.560
And you'll need that,
because I want you to see

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.329
how these shells dissolve.

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:46.899
And if you lower
the acidity more--

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:48.167
we don't want to wait 40--

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:49.902
well, you can wait
45 days, but I

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:52.972
want you to be able to do
these experiments in minutes.

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:57.610
So we're going to go a little
lower in acidity, in ph,

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:59.445
but you're going to
get the same result.

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:01.013
You're going to
dissolve these shells.

00:08:05.852 --> 00:08:09.021
Some of you are going
on planes, and there's

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:14.126
white powder and a scale
involved in this goodie bag.

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:18.831
Please don't put that
in your carry-on.

00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:19.832
It's just not a good--

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:21.701
it really isn't a good idea.

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:27.006
We have data-- students sending
me pictures with the airport

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:29.709
screening person.

00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:33.546
So I love the
excitement of thinking

00:08:33.546 --> 00:08:35.948
about this over Thanksgiving,
but put it in your suitcase

00:08:35.948 --> 00:08:37.250
if you're going to take it home.

00:08:39.986 --> 00:08:43.456
That's my why this matters.

00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:45.358
So we're dissolving stuff today.

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:46.325
That's the topic.

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:48.327
How do things dissolve?

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:52.064
There's a citric
powder, that citric acid

00:08:52.064 --> 00:08:55.434
that's going to make
water more acidic.

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:58.170
And again, we're going to move
into acids and bases next.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.339
Today we're just talking
about dissolution.

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:02.475
And so here's citric acid.

00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:03.142
There it is.

00:09:03.142 --> 00:09:05.278
Now over Thanksgiving, I
thought I'd throw this in,

00:09:05.278 --> 00:09:07.613
because you might have
sugar at the table.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:10.950
You know to call it a c12h22o11.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.285
Don't ask for the sugar.

00:09:12.285 --> 00:09:14.053
If you want to add some
sugar to something,

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:16.789
ask for it by molecular name.

00:09:16.789 --> 00:09:18.424
But the question is
why do these things

00:09:18.424 --> 00:09:20.626
dissolve in the first place.

00:09:20.626 --> 00:09:26.032
There is acetic acid,
citric acid, there's sugar.

00:09:26.032 --> 00:09:27.900
These are molecules
that look like that.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.269
Why do they dissolve
in the first place?

00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:35.040
And the answer is
because of the things

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.045
that we learned about
bonding and about things

00:09:39.045 --> 00:09:43.783
like whether a molecule
is polar or not.

00:09:43.783 --> 00:09:47.219
Or in this case, whether
you've got the possibility

00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:49.589
to hydrogen bond or not.

00:09:52.391 --> 00:09:54.460
And so if you look
at both of these,

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:59.332
you can see that they both
have a whole bunch of oh groups

00:09:59.332 --> 00:10:02.735
that look a whole lot
like things we've seen

00:10:02.735 --> 00:10:04.971
that like to hydrogen bond.

00:10:04.971 --> 00:10:06.839
You see those ohs
on the end there,

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:12.912
both for sugar, around the
outside, and for citric acid.

00:10:12.912 --> 00:10:17.984
And so what happens is if I put
a sugar molecule into water,

00:10:17.984 --> 00:10:20.219
here's a picture of it.

00:10:20.219 --> 00:10:21.621
There's a picture of it.

00:10:21.621 --> 00:10:23.289
So there's sugar
molecules there.

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:26.826
Now they start in a
crystal of sugar--

00:10:26.826 --> 00:10:30.296
you pour your sugar in,
a teaspoon of sugar.

00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:34.266
And it's in a solid,
but then it goes off,

00:10:34.266 --> 00:10:36.836
and it breaks away
into the water.

00:10:36.836 --> 00:10:39.672
And those little ones
are the water molecules.

00:10:39.672 --> 00:10:40.306
Why?

00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:44.010
Because the water was
bonding to itself.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.911
The water has dipoles--

00:10:45.911 --> 00:10:46.545
dipole-dipole.

00:10:46.545 --> 00:10:47.113
Remember that?

00:10:47.113 --> 00:10:54.320
The water has hydrogen bonds,
but the sugar does, too.

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:55.688
The sugar does, too.

00:10:55.688 --> 00:10:58.691
And so the water sees
this crystal of sugar,

00:10:58.691 --> 00:11:00.426
and it says well, hang on.

00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:04.897
If you come out, we could bond
together in much the same way

00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:06.699
that we're already
bonding with each other.

00:11:06.699 --> 00:11:09.201
The water molecules are already
bonding with hydrogen dipole.

00:11:09.201 --> 00:11:12.338
The sugar is like, well I've
got hydrogen. I've got dipole.

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:16.809
And so it can break off, and it
doesn't cost that much energy.

00:11:16.809 --> 00:11:20.780
In fact, it gains
energy by doing this.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:21.681
They can gain.

00:11:21.681 --> 00:11:27.453
And so this can actually be
a lower energy state overall.

00:11:27.453 --> 00:11:35.428
Now that's because of
something that many of you

00:11:35.428 --> 00:11:40.933
have probably heard, and now
we can put some bonding to it,

00:11:40.933 --> 00:11:45.838
but it's because
like dissolves like.

00:11:45.838 --> 00:11:47.373
Oh yeah.

00:11:47.373 --> 00:11:48.340
That's technical.

00:11:51.143 --> 00:11:53.846
That's technical because
now we know what like means.

00:11:53.846 --> 00:11:56.615
Like means a type of body.

00:11:56.615 --> 00:11:58.417
like means a type of--

00:11:58.417 --> 00:11:59.719
It's like water.

00:11:59.719 --> 00:12:02.088
It has hydrogen
bonding potential.

00:12:02.088 --> 00:12:06.158
Therefore, it's able to
dissolve in the water.

00:12:09.028 --> 00:12:13.232
So water has-- well,
what does water have?

00:12:13.232 --> 00:12:17.336
It's got dipoles, so it
can do dipole-dipole stuff.

00:12:17.336 --> 00:12:19.171
It's got h-bonds.

00:12:19.171 --> 00:12:22.675
Oh, everything has London.

00:12:22.675 --> 00:12:26.112
Everything has London.

00:12:26.112 --> 00:12:27.580
So if you've got
those ingredients,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.782
if you've got that
ability, if you've got

00:12:29.782 --> 00:12:32.218
those likes, then you
might be able to dissolve.

00:12:36.422 --> 00:12:45.197
Well, this is called
a polar solvent

00:12:45.197 --> 00:12:48.167
because the molecules
that make it up are polar.

00:12:50.736 --> 00:12:51.871
They've got dipole.

00:12:51.871 --> 00:12:55.107
Actually, and so we're very
interested in understanding

00:12:55.107 --> 00:12:57.243
how things mix with
other things and why

00:12:57.243 --> 00:12:59.378
they mix with other things.

00:12:59.378 --> 00:13:02.348
Let's take a look
at another example.

00:13:02.348 --> 00:13:03.549
So here we have ethanol.

00:13:03.549 --> 00:13:05.417
I keep on bringing up ethanol.

00:13:05.417 --> 00:13:08.420
There might be some served,
and if you're 21 or over,

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:09.922
you can have some.

00:13:09.922 --> 00:13:13.025
Otherwise, it's illegal.

00:13:13.025 --> 00:13:15.161
Ethanol up there at the top.

00:13:15.161 --> 00:13:18.531
Thank goodness, you will say,
at your Thanksgiving table,

00:13:18.531 --> 00:13:21.000
it is not methoxymethane.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.103
And because if it were, it would
be the same chemical formula,

00:13:24.103 --> 00:13:25.337
but the oh--

00:13:25.337 --> 00:13:27.406
the o would be in the
middle of the two cs

00:13:27.406 --> 00:13:29.575
with the ch3s on the
end, and you wouldn't

00:13:29.575 --> 00:13:30.743
be able to hydrogen bond it.

00:13:30.743 --> 00:13:32.678
It would all just be vapor.

00:13:32.678 --> 00:13:34.180
It'd be vapor.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:37.283
No, but it's ethanol because
it has hydrogen bonding

00:13:37.283 --> 00:13:37.950
capabilities.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:39.952
But look at what happens
if it's heptanol.

00:13:39.952 --> 00:13:42.822
So all I've done now is I've
taken the same end group,

00:13:42.822 --> 00:13:48.093
and I've given it more
of a carbon chain.

00:13:48.093 --> 00:13:50.796
How is that going to
affect how it dissolves?

00:13:50.796 --> 00:13:52.398
Given what we just
talked about that

00:13:52.398 --> 00:13:57.203
like dissolve-- they both
have hydrogen bond potential.

00:13:57.203 --> 00:14:00.372
They both have hy-- but
look, more and more of it,

00:14:00.372 --> 00:14:03.375
as you go from ethanol to
heptanol, more and more

00:14:03.375 --> 00:14:05.678
and more of it is
something else.

00:14:05.678 --> 00:14:09.348
It's only London.

00:14:09.348 --> 00:14:12.384
So what's going to happen
now if I put this into water?

00:14:12.384 --> 00:14:14.887
Well, we can see
this going series.

00:14:14.887 --> 00:14:19.091
Here's methanol, ethanol,
propanol, butanol, pentanol,

00:14:19.091 --> 00:14:19.892
hexanol.

00:14:19.892 --> 00:14:22.061
Heptanol is down at
the bottom, and there's

00:14:22.061 --> 00:14:24.697
the water solubility.

00:14:24.697 --> 00:14:25.998
We've got to write that down--

00:14:25.998 --> 00:14:27.099
solubility.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.741
So solubility is kind
of what it sounds like--

00:14:35.741 --> 00:14:40.379
the ability for a solute--

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:42.081
that's the thing
we're dissolving--

00:14:45.117 --> 00:14:52.358
to dissolve in a solvent.

00:14:52.358 --> 00:14:56.228
That's the thing we're
trying to dissolve it into.

00:14:56.228 --> 00:14:58.697
That's the solubility.

00:14:58.697 --> 00:15:00.966
And we'll be talking about
the maximum solubility

00:15:00.966 --> 00:15:03.535
in just a few minutes,
which is what we usually--

00:15:03.535 --> 00:15:04.837
what's the solubility?

00:15:04.837 --> 00:15:07.640
We often mean the maximum.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:09.174
Well, that's what's
listed here--

00:15:09.174 --> 00:15:09.675
the maximum.

00:15:12.544 --> 00:15:14.480
Methanol, ethanol miscible.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:17.783
So you can put as much as
you want, and it'll always

00:15:17.783 --> 00:15:20.085
keep mixing in with the water.

00:15:20.085 --> 00:15:23.522
But now look, once you get to
butanol, pentanol, hexanol,

00:15:23.522 --> 00:15:25.724
it starts going down.

00:15:25.724 --> 00:15:29.028
You can't mix as
much as you want.

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:30.829
In fact, it's less
and less and less,

00:15:30.829 --> 00:15:35.668
and the reason we now know is
because of what I just said.

00:15:35.668 --> 00:15:39.872
As you go down, the hydrogen
bond and dipole-dipole

00:15:39.872 --> 00:15:41.807
becomes less important
because you've

00:15:41.807 --> 00:15:46.478
got more and more of the
molecule that's only London.

00:15:46.478 --> 00:15:51.183
And so it's less like the
water environment, the bonding

00:15:51.183 --> 00:15:55.788
environment that the
water liked with itself.

00:15:55.788 --> 00:15:59.058
And so it has more
trouble dissolving

00:15:59.058 --> 00:16:01.026
and being
energetically favorable

00:16:01.026 --> 00:16:02.594
isolated by those
water monitors.

00:16:02.594 --> 00:16:06.131
By the way, does anybody know
the origin of the word proof,

00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:08.600
speaking of ethanol?

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:13.739
If you buy a bottle of
ethanol at the supermarket--

00:16:13.739 --> 00:16:16.976
so there's gin or
vodka or whatever,

00:16:16.976 --> 00:16:19.645
and it says it's something
percent alcohol--

00:16:19.645 --> 00:16:22.314
20% alcohol, 40 proof.

00:16:22.314 --> 00:16:23.983
Does anybody know
where that comes from?

00:16:23.983 --> 00:16:25.351
It's this.

00:16:25.351 --> 00:16:27.586
It's just-- but
what they did, they

00:16:27.586 --> 00:16:30.756
didn't know if you were making
good alcohol back in the time.

00:16:30.756 --> 00:16:33.058
And they said,
well, I don't have

00:16:33.058 --> 00:16:35.861
all these detailed abilities
to measure and quantify it,

00:16:35.861 --> 00:16:38.931
so I was going to
light it on fire.

00:16:38.931 --> 00:16:41.033
That is exactly what--

00:16:41.033 --> 00:16:42.835
is this a good bottle of gin?

00:16:42.835 --> 00:16:43.635
I don't know.

00:16:43.635 --> 00:16:44.870
Let's light it on fire.

00:16:47.406 --> 00:16:48.807
That's what they did.

00:16:48.807 --> 00:16:54.046
And it turns out that if ethanol
and water are at 50/50 mixture

00:16:54.046 --> 00:16:57.349
or more, it will light on fire.

00:16:57.349 --> 00:16:59.318
Don't try that, please.

00:16:59.318 --> 00:17:01.487
Don't do these experiments.

00:17:01.487 --> 00:17:06.191
But so that meant is this
a good bottle of gin?

00:17:06.191 --> 00:17:09.862
Well if it's 50/50, I'm going to
say it's a good bottle of gin.

00:17:09.862 --> 00:17:11.096
So let's light it on fire.

00:17:11.096 --> 00:17:12.464
Oh, it lit on fire.

00:17:12.464 --> 00:17:16.468
That's 100% proof
that it's good.

00:17:16.468 --> 00:17:18.137
That is true.

00:17:18.137 --> 00:17:21.807
That is the origin
of the word proof.

00:17:21.807 --> 00:17:25.844
It is proof that it's mixed
well, and it's good stuff.

00:17:29.882 --> 00:17:33.952
Dissolution-- dissolution--
well, what else

00:17:33.952 --> 00:17:36.121
can we try to dissolve?

00:17:36.121 --> 00:17:37.589
We're dissolving alcohol.

00:17:37.589 --> 00:17:38.791
We're going down to heptanol.

00:17:38.791 --> 00:17:42.194
We're lucky it's
not methoxymethane.

00:17:42.194 --> 00:17:44.163
We talked about sugar.

00:17:44.163 --> 00:17:47.332
So we've got to have a
framework, by the way.

00:17:47.332 --> 00:17:50.402
Solubility-- the ability
for a solute to dissolve.

00:17:50.402 --> 00:17:53.305
And now, there's a
context here, right?

00:17:53.305 --> 00:17:55.774
Let's suppose I'm
dissolving sugar.

00:17:55.774 --> 00:18:01.713
So sugar-- how are we going
to write this dissolving down?

00:18:01.713 --> 00:18:11.824
Well, sugar is c12h22o11, and
if we put it in water, we could

00:18:11.824 --> 00:18:14.960
we could formally write
the whole thing out.

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:15.861
We can write it out.

00:18:15.861 --> 00:18:23.469
We can say I'm going
to add water to it,

00:18:23.469 --> 00:18:26.205
and that's going to go to--

00:18:26.205 --> 00:18:32.377
it's going to go to
c12h22o11 plus more water.

00:18:32.377 --> 00:18:36.548
But there's something very
important that I'm leaving out,

00:18:36.548 --> 00:18:38.050
and when you think
about dissolution,

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:40.486
you've got to keep track.

00:18:40.486 --> 00:18:41.386
What was it?

00:18:41.386 --> 00:18:42.721
Was it a solid?

00:18:42.721 --> 00:18:43.622
Was it a liquid?

00:18:43.622 --> 00:18:47.126
Or was it something
dissolved in a liquid?

00:18:47.126 --> 00:18:50.362
So this started as a solid,
so we put an s there.

00:18:50.362 --> 00:18:52.064
This started as a liquid.

00:18:52.064 --> 00:18:55.801
This is the dissolved
thing, so we put an aq.

00:18:55.801 --> 00:18:58.036
And the aq means aqueous.

00:18:58.036 --> 00:18:59.671
It is in an aqueous solution.

00:18:59.671 --> 00:19:01.373
It is a dissolved molecule.

00:19:04.476 --> 00:19:05.978
And so here, this is a liquid.

00:19:05.978 --> 00:19:11.583
Now the other thing
that happens is-- well,

00:19:11.583 --> 00:19:15.254
the h2o is kind of there.

00:19:15.254 --> 00:19:16.622
It's all over the place.

00:19:16.622 --> 00:19:20.292
It's that solvent,
and so we often

00:19:20.292 --> 00:19:24.630
leave it out because it's
everywhere on both sides.

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:26.431
And so often, what
you'll see, and we'll

00:19:26.431 --> 00:19:29.801
write more of these coming,
is that you might write

00:19:29.801 --> 00:19:34.306
a dissolution of sugar as this.

00:19:34.306 --> 00:19:39.378
Solid, and it just goes to
something that is dissolved.

00:19:44.917 --> 00:19:47.486
And you know that what
happened there is sometimes

00:19:47.486 --> 00:19:49.321
you'll also see it like this.

00:19:49.321 --> 00:19:50.622
What did you do on the arrow?

00:19:50.622 --> 00:19:52.591
I added water.

00:19:52.591 --> 00:19:55.827
That's what the arrow did.

00:19:55.827 --> 00:19:59.598
We're just getting comfortable
with writing these dissolution

00:19:59.598 --> 00:20:00.732
reactions.

00:20:00.732 --> 00:20:02.034
Now what else can I dissolve?

00:20:02.034 --> 00:20:06.271
Well here's another
good example--

00:20:06.271 --> 00:20:10.008
vitamin A. There's some
vitamins you can have as much

00:20:10.008 --> 00:20:11.543
as you want, pretty much.

00:20:11.543 --> 00:20:12.177
Why?

00:20:12.177 --> 00:20:14.913
Because they're water-soluble.

00:20:14.913 --> 00:20:16.682
And other vitamins
are fat-soluble.

00:20:16.682 --> 00:20:21.353
That means they
don't like water as

00:20:21.353 --> 00:20:27.693
much as they like the
hydrophobic molecules

00:20:27.693 --> 00:20:28.760
of fat cells.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:31.830
And so they go
into fatty tissue,

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:34.499
where they don't come out.

00:20:34.499 --> 00:20:38.804
That's why you not have
unlimited amounts of vitamin

00:20:38.804 --> 00:20:41.440
A. That's not good for you.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:44.076
But vitamin C-- no problem.

00:20:44.076 --> 00:20:48.647
No problem, because it's
going to dissolve in water.

00:20:48.647 --> 00:20:51.250
And so your body can take in
and get rid of as much of it

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:51.750
as you want.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:53.885
But if it's dissolved
in fat cells,

00:20:53.885 --> 00:20:54.953
that's a different thing.

00:20:54.953 --> 00:20:57.055
So vitamin A,
vitamin C-- and you

00:20:57.055 --> 00:20:59.124
can see it right there
from the chemistry.

00:20:59.124 --> 00:20:59.658
This is it.

00:20:59.658 --> 00:21:02.261
Look at all those oh
groups in vitamin c.

00:21:02.261 --> 00:21:03.362
Look at that.

00:21:03.362 --> 00:21:06.265
Oh, that's going to
love hydrogen bonding,

00:21:06.265 --> 00:21:09.301
and on vitamin A, you've just
got the one all the way down

00:21:09.301 --> 00:21:11.103
there at the bottom.

00:21:11.103 --> 00:21:13.372
It's hydrophobic.

00:21:13.372 --> 00:21:16.074
By the way, in this
class, we do not

00:21:16.074 --> 00:21:17.843
say the words
hydrophobic interaction

00:21:17.843 --> 00:21:19.411
because that doesn't make sense.

00:21:19.411 --> 00:21:23.415
There is no such thing as
a hydrophobic interaction.

00:21:23.415 --> 00:21:28.687
No, hydrophobic
interaction is misused.

00:21:28.687 --> 00:21:31.490
What it really means is that
something that's hydrophobic

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:33.925
likes itself more--

00:21:33.925 --> 00:21:36.395
being together with
itself-- more than it

00:21:36.395 --> 00:21:40.098
likes being in the water,
or something hydrophilic.

00:21:40.098 --> 00:21:43.502
That's what is meant, but
there's no actual interaction

00:21:43.502 --> 00:21:45.537
there.

00:21:45.537 --> 00:21:48.573
Hydrophobic, hydrophilic.

00:21:48.573 --> 00:21:51.877
Well, those are other things
you can think about dissolving.

00:21:51.877 --> 00:21:56.048
You can also dissolve
salt, and this is what

00:21:56.048 --> 00:21:59.017
we're going to use as examples.

00:21:59.017 --> 00:22:01.019
And this is a picture
of a salt crystal.

00:22:01.019 --> 00:22:04.489
So we show the sugar,
and here's salt. Now,

00:22:04.489 --> 00:22:05.824
so the thing about salt--

00:22:05.824 --> 00:22:06.591
where's my sugar?

00:22:06.591 --> 00:22:10.862
There's the sugar, and let's
put salt underneath it.

00:22:10.862 --> 00:22:14.566
The thing about
salt, which is really

00:22:14.566 --> 00:22:18.737
just a general term for when
you have an ionic solid that

00:22:18.737 --> 00:22:22.341
can be made from a mixture of
acids and bases and dissolves

00:22:22.341 --> 00:22:26.345
oftentimes a little
bit, at least in water.

00:22:26.345 --> 00:22:32.851
So a salt or an ionic solid--

00:22:32.851 --> 00:22:33.852
let's say for now--

00:22:37.422 --> 00:22:41.593
this gives something different,
because this breaks apart

00:22:41.593 --> 00:22:51.570
into positive and negative
ions that they contain.

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:55.140
So in the case of sodium and
chlorine-- sodium chloride--

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:56.675
well, that's going
to dissociate.

00:22:56.675 --> 00:23:00.979
If you pour salt in water, then
here's the reaction for that.

00:23:00.979 --> 00:23:03.515
You've got sodium
chloride solid.

00:23:03.515 --> 00:23:06.017
We put it in water.

00:23:06.017 --> 00:23:07.285
That's going to go into ions.

00:23:14.893 --> 00:23:18.130
So I put that in water, and
it's different in the sense

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:21.299
that it doesn't break down
into the molecules that

00:23:21.299 --> 00:23:22.968
made up the sugar.

00:23:22.968 --> 00:23:24.603
It has a particle
of sugar that's

00:23:24.603 --> 00:23:27.172
got millions and millions
of molecules in it.

00:23:27.172 --> 00:23:28.640
Now they dissociate.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:31.443
That's what the aq means.

00:23:31.443 --> 00:23:32.477
Those are neutral.

00:23:32.477 --> 00:23:36.648
Here, it's a solid, but
you can understand this

00:23:36.648 --> 00:23:38.316
from looking at it
and thinking about it.

00:23:38.316 --> 00:23:41.520
Look at the way those water
molecules are oriented.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:45.791
They're pointing to the
cl with their positivity,

00:23:45.791 --> 00:23:47.259
with positive charge.

00:23:47.259 --> 00:23:50.395
And they're pointing to the
sodium with the negative,

00:23:50.395 --> 00:23:53.865
and that's how they find
happiness in water--

00:23:53.865 --> 00:23:58.236
because they're ions, and
water can be either way.

00:23:58.236 --> 00:24:00.272
It can point its
dipole in either--

00:24:00.272 --> 00:24:01.506
and so it can actually bond.

00:24:01.506 --> 00:24:03.775
Remember, the hydrogen has
got a little positive charge

00:24:03.775 --> 00:24:06.344
because the oxygen took it.

00:24:06.344 --> 00:24:08.547
And so that's what
the-- it has a dipole,

00:24:08.547 --> 00:24:11.082
and that's able to
turn around and bond

00:24:11.082 --> 00:24:12.951
and be happy with
each of these ions.

00:24:12.951 --> 00:24:14.486
That's why salt dissolves--

00:24:17.722 --> 00:24:20.358
because it can
find a happy place.

00:24:20.358 --> 00:24:22.694
Other reasons exist
outside of this class,

00:24:22.694 --> 00:24:27.232
like entropy, thermodynamics.

00:24:27.232 --> 00:24:29.701
But this is how we're going
to picture it as energetically

00:24:29.701 --> 00:24:30.268
happy.

00:24:30.268 --> 00:24:31.470
It can become happy.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:37.108
So we've dissolved some things.

00:24:37.108 --> 00:24:39.544
We've got to start
thinking about the thing

00:24:39.544 --> 00:24:43.915
that I mentioned before, which
is how much can I dissolve?

00:24:43.915 --> 00:24:45.650
How much can I dissolve?

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:50.789
And in that, it gets
into this table here.

00:24:50.789 --> 00:24:51.423
There it is.

00:24:51.423 --> 00:24:53.258
So that's how much.

00:24:53.258 --> 00:24:58.263
And you see it's listed
there as moles per 100 grams.

00:24:58.263 --> 00:25:00.031
And so that's one
way to write it.

00:25:00.031 --> 00:25:02.667
You could write it as--

00:25:02.667 --> 00:25:14.279
it's often the max solubility is
often what we're interested in.

00:25:14.279 --> 00:25:18.984
And you could write it
in moles per 100 grams.

00:25:18.984 --> 00:25:23.088
You could write it
in moles per liter.

00:25:23.088 --> 00:25:26.258
Well that's something that
we've already learned.

00:25:26.258 --> 00:25:30.595
That's m form last
Friday, the molarity.

00:25:30.595 --> 00:25:33.698
Or it could be in grams.

00:25:33.698 --> 00:25:36.401
Sometimes you'll see
it in grams per liter.

00:25:36.401 --> 00:25:40.639
So don't get bogged
down by the units.

00:25:40.639 --> 00:25:47.412
It's just how much of this
stuff were you able to dissolve?

00:25:47.412 --> 00:25:49.180
If we look at sugar--

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:50.982
here's sugar, here's salt--

00:25:50.982 --> 00:25:52.284
how much sugar can dissolve?

00:25:59.524 --> 00:26:00.792
I'm not going to cram it in.

00:26:03.595 --> 00:26:13.872
So for sugar, the
max is 1,800, so I'm

00:26:13.872 --> 00:26:16.007
going to do both those units--

00:26:16.007 --> 00:26:18.777
1,800 grams per liter.

00:26:18.777 --> 00:26:24.082
And if you convert that,
it's 5.26 moles per liter.

00:26:24.082 --> 00:26:27.886
That's how much sugar you
can dissolve in water.

00:26:27.886 --> 00:26:29.721
By the way, I did a
quick calculation,

00:26:29.721 --> 00:26:33.959
and that's 429 teaspoons.

00:26:33.959 --> 00:26:36.528
That's 429 teaspoons.

00:26:36.528 --> 00:26:38.430
I don't know what
everybody is saying--

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:40.765
Coke has so much sugar.

00:26:40.765 --> 00:26:45.704
Coke's got only 26
teaspoons per liter.

00:26:45.704 --> 00:26:49.040
You could put a lot
more in, if they wanted.

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:50.275
I'll go the other way.

00:26:50.275 --> 00:26:55.780
I challenge them to go
near the solubility limit.

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:57.949
They're not even close.

00:26:57.949 --> 00:27:00.752
Too much sugar.

00:27:00.752 --> 00:27:07.459
So salt-- you can also look
at salt. For salt, what is it?

00:27:07.459 --> 00:27:12.497
Salt is 359 grams per
liter, and then that's it.

00:27:12.497 --> 00:27:15.233
That's 6.14.

00:27:15.233 --> 00:27:15.800
This is nacl.

00:27:20.772 --> 00:27:23.942
It's interesting-- if you
compare sodium chloride with

00:27:23.942 --> 00:27:26.177
sodium bromide--

00:27:26.177 --> 00:27:35.487
so if you look at sodium
bromide, it's going to be 8.9.

00:27:35.487 --> 00:27:39.524
And sodium fluoride
there's only going to be 1.

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.294
Why?

00:27:43.294 --> 00:27:46.464
Because it comes right
down to the picture.

00:27:46.464 --> 00:27:49.334
I've got to make these happier.

00:27:49.334 --> 00:27:50.835
I've got to make them happier.

00:27:50.835 --> 00:27:53.571
If you've got the ions there,
and the water likes it,

00:27:53.571 --> 00:27:56.875
but what if they really didn't
have a strong ionic bond

00:27:56.875 --> 00:27:58.009
to start with?

00:27:58.009 --> 00:28:00.979
You can imagine you might
be able to get to a higher

00:28:00.979 --> 00:28:02.580
amount of dissociation.

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:05.917
Or what if they had a
very strong ionic bond?

00:28:05.917 --> 00:28:07.519
You might imagine
that less of it--

00:28:07.519 --> 00:28:10.021
you've got a lower
maximum solubility.

00:28:10.021 --> 00:28:12.991
So it all kind of goes
together with the things

00:28:12.991 --> 00:28:13.792
that we've learned.

00:28:18.029 --> 00:28:20.598
But what we're
really talking about

00:28:20.598 --> 00:28:22.967
is what I need to
tell you about next.

00:28:22.967 --> 00:28:28.473
And that is this idea that we've
reached a dynamic equilibrium.

00:28:28.473 --> 00:28:30.842
We've reached a
dynamic equilibrium.

00:28:30.842 --> 00:28:32.610
So what does that mean?

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:35.113
Well, if it's unsaturated--

00:28:35.113 --> 00:28:39.584
so if it's unsaturated,
I'm way before this limit.

00:28:39.584 --> 00:28:43.455
This is salt. That's
the limit max.

00:28:43.455 --> 00:28:46.024
Max.

00:28:46.024 --> 00:28:48.893
What does it mean?

00:28:48.893 --> 00:28:51.429
What does it mean
to be below it?

00:28:51.429 --> 00:28:54.032
Well, what does it mean to
even have reached the maximum

00:28:54.032 --> 00:28:54.532
itself?

00:28:57.102 --> 00:29:00.505
What it means is not that
things aren't dissolving.

00:29:00.505 --> 00:29:04.542
What it means is that things
are reforming, recrystallizing

00:29:04.542 --> 00:29:07.679
at the same rate that
they're dissolving.

00:29:07.679 --> 00:29:11.850
That's what the maximum means,
and that's an equilibrium.

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.752
And that's why we're
going to talk about next.

00:29:13.752 --> 00:29:16.421
So if it's unsaturated,
there's a nice little molecular

00:29:16.421 --> 00:29:17.055
picture.

00:29:17.055 --> 00:29:19.824
There's the thing you're
dissolving, and look at them.

00:29:19.824 --> 00:29:22.460
They're going away
into solution.

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:24.729
Maybe it's salt
breaking up into ions.

00:29:24.729 --> 00:29:26.731
Maybe it's sugar.

00:29:26.731 --> 00:29:28.967
They're going into solution
and finding a happy place

00:29:28.967 --> 00:29:29.701
around the water.

00:29:29.701 --> 00:29:31.870
But once in a while,
one of them comes back.

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:34.405
You see that little
particle there coming back.

00:29:34.405 --> 00:29:35.774
That happens.

00:29:35.774 --> 00:29:39.344
But much more,
they're dissolving.

00:29:39.344 --> 00:29:43.948
Then, you reach this point of
saturation where it cannot--

00:29:43.948 --> 00:29:46.417
where it's going
to recrystallize,

00:29:46.417 --> 00:29:49.521
reform the solid at the
same rate that you dissolve.

00:29:49.521 --> 00:29:55.627
And that's the place where
you can't dissolve anymore.

00:29:55.627 --> 00:29:57.862
So what's really
happening is that you

00:29:57.862 --> 00:30:04.969
have a solute plus a solvent,
and it's not going one way

00:30:04.969 --> 00:30:08.072
as we've been writing it down.

00:30:08.072 --> 00:30:09.674
It's going both ways.

00:30:09.674 --> 00:30:13.244
So it's actually the solute
plus the solvent goes like this.

00:30:13.244 --> 00:30:14.813
That is what we've
been talking about.

00:30:14.813 --> 00:30:19.717
That's dissolution, and that's
the predominant mechanism

00:30:19.717 --> 00:30:22.620
on the left before
you've saturated.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:26.124
But see, and that
goes to some solution.

00:30:26.124 --> 00:30:28.193
But see, you can
also precipitate.

00:30:36.167 --> 00:30:39.337
Precipitation.

00:30:39.337 --> 00:30:42.874
And so at the saturated
solution, you have--

00:30:42.874 --> 00:30:49.280
if it's saturated, then that's
another way of saying we've

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:59.290
reached the max possible
concentration of the solute.

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:05.363
Remember, the little brackets
means concentration, molarity.

00:31:05.363 --> 00:31:08.499
It means that these rates--

00:31:08.499 --> 00:31:10.368
thinking back to Friday--

00:31:10.368 --> 00:31:12.403
are the same.

00:31:12.403 --> 00:31:15.340
It means these
rates are the same.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:18.076
This is a dynamic equilibrium.

00:31:18.076 --> 00:31:21.479
And so that's what we need
now the tools to talk about.

00:31:21.479 --> 00:31:25.516
And so we're going to
talk about three things--

00:31:25.516 --> 00:31:30.188
the reaction quotient, the
equilibrium constant k,

00:31:30.188 --> 00:31:33.791
chemical equilibrium, and
then the solubility product.

00:31:33.791 --> 00:31:36.294
Now if you think about--

00:31:36.294 --> 00:31:38.363
I just said this is like
the forward reaction is

00:31:38.363 --> 00:31:40.665
going in the same--

00:31:40.665 --> 00:31:43.301
it's happening in the
same rate as the reverse.

00:31:43.301 --> 00:31:47.972
And so if you think about
this as like a graph

00:31:47.972 --> 00:31:50.808
of concentrations--

00:31:50.808 --> 00:31:56.648
so I'll just put here
concentrations and time--

00:31:56.648 --> 00:31:58.783
then maybe I have started--

00:31:58.783 --> 00:32:04.422
I've put my salt. I pour a
teaspoon of salt in water,

00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:09.227
and the salt is dissolving.

00:32:09.227 --> 00:32:11.629
So it's going into the products.

00:32:11.629 --> 00:32:14.165
It starts out at
zero in solution,

00:32:14.165 --> 00:32:17.602
and then it becomes products.

00:32:22.473 --> 00:32:26.577
At the same time, maybe
I've started it up here,

00:32:26.577 --> 00:32:27.779
and these are the reactants--

00:32:27.779 --> 00:32:30.214
Gesundheit-- that
look like this--

00:32:30.214 --> 00:32:32.517
Gesundheit-- and those
would be the reactants.

00:32:35.353 --> 00:32:37.956
So the concentrations
of these things--

00:32:37.956 --> 00:32:41.292
at a certain point,
they're changing.

00:32:41.292 --> 00:32:44.929
And then, at a certain
point, they're not changing.

00:32:44.929 --> 00:32:48.232
But they're still reactions
happening back and forth

00:32:48.232 --> 00:32:49.167
like I just showed you.

00:32:49.167 --> 00:32:52.570
It's a dynamic equilibrium.

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:56.207
Now what we do is we write down
for some arbitrary reaction--

00:32:56.207 --> 00:32:59.644
aa plus, so let's
go back to this one.

00:32:59.644 --> 00:33:02.714
But now, we're going to
write it going both ways--

00:33:02.714 --> 00:33:05.950
cc plus dd.

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:08.486
You can define the
reaction quotient q--

00:33:08.486 --> 00:33:10.154
that's the first thing--

00:33:10.154 --> 00:33:15.193
as the concentrations
c of the products--

00:33:15.193 --> 00:33:25.703
c to the c, d to the d,
over a to the a, b to the b.

00:33:25.703 --> 00:33:30.341
Please do not confuse
this with the rates.

00:33:30.341 --> 00:33:30.842
Yeah.

00:33:30.842 --> 00:33:33.478
I've been talking about
the rates being equal.

00:33:33.478 --> 00:33:35.179
That's not from the rates.

00:33:35.179 --> 00:33:39.250
Remember, the exponents
of the rate law

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:43.688
come from experimental
measurements.

00:33:43.688 --> 00:33:47.058
The reaction quotient comes
from the stoichiometric

00:33:47.058 --> 00:33:49.861
coefficients.

00:33:49.861 --> 00:33:50.862
Why?

00:33:50.862 --> 00:33:55.933
These exponents here have to
do literally with probabilities

00:33:55.933 --> 00:33:59.137
of reaction, of reacting.

00:33:59.137 --> 00:34:03.875
It has to do with the fact
that if I've got two of c,

00:34:03.875 --> 00:34:08.780
then you multiply the amount
of c times another amount of c

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:10.281
in the same volume.

00:34:10.281 --> 00:34:12.683
It has to do with
probabilities of reacting.

00:34:12.683 --> 00:34:14.418
That's where you
get those exponents,

00:34:14.418 --> 00:34:17.655
and they're directly from the
stoichiometric coefficients.

00:34:17.655 --> 00:34:21.125
They do not need to be
experimentally measured.

00:34:21.125 --> 00:34:24.128
Once you have this, you
have the reaction quotient.

00:34:26.764 --> 00:34:29.500
Now, why is this useful,
this reaction quotient?

00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:34.338
Well, it's useful because there
is a very special place where

00:34:34.338 --> 00:34:39.543
now it doesn't change anymore.

00:34:39.543 --> 00:34:41.212
These concentrations
aren't changing.

00:34:41.212 --> 00:34:43.915
Reactions are still happening,
but the concentrations

00:34:43.915 --> 00:34:46.551
aren't changing-- no change.

00:34:49.821 --> 00:34:52.723
If there is no change, then--

00:34:52.723 --> 00:34:55.927
Gesundheit-- then q isn't
changing because it's just

00:34:55.927 --> 00:34:58.463
a ratio of concentrations.

00:34:58.463 --> 00:35:03.034
It's a ratio of concentrations
equal to this whole thing, not

00:35:03.034 --> 00:35:05.870
just the top line.

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:11.509
The reaction quotient is
related to the concentrations.

00:35:11.509 --> 00:35:13.010
So if the concentration
zone changes

00:35:13.010 --> 00:35:16.214
even if reactions are happening
still, it's a constant.

00:35:16.214 --> 00:35:21.919
So at this point, q
equals a constant,

00:35:21.919 --> 00:35:25.123
and that is an
equilibrium constant.

00:35:25.123 --> 00:35:26.023
So we'll call it keq.

00:35:30.094 --> 00:35:32.130
It's a dynamic equilibrium.

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:36.868
k represents a
dynamic equilibrium.

00:35:36.868 --> 00:35:42.707
And what's really useful about
k is that for a given reaction,

00:35:42.707 --> 00:35:44.509
k is a constant.

00:35:44.509 --> 00:35:45.643
I just said it.

00:35:45.643 --> 00:35:47.478
But it doesn't
matter-- if we're--

00:35:47.478 --> 00:35:49.680
it depends on temperature.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:53.084
But if I keep the temperature
fixed, k won't change.

00:35:53.084 --> 00:35:55.620
It's a constant.

00:35:55.620 --> 00:36:01.292
It's the constant that tells
me where equilibrium is.

00:36:01.292 --> 00:36:03.494
It's the constant that tells
me where equilibrium is.

00:36:07.064 --> 00:36:10.268
Now there's one more thing
that we've got to write down--

00:36:10.268 --> 00:36:11.536
that third one there.

00:36:11.536 --> 00:36:12.703
I'm going to put it on here.

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:20.778
So k is an equilibrium constant.

00:36:20.778 --> 00:36:22.246
That's a general constant.

00:36:22.246 --> 00:36:24.515
But now, I'm going
to turn my attention

00:36:24.515 --> 00:36:27.118
from any old reaction--

00:36:27.118 --> 00:36:30.121
my k is actually very general
concept for equilibrium.

00:36:30.121 --> 00:36:35.026
We are in this class going
to use it for solubility.

00:36:35.026 --> 00:36:37.495
That's how we are using
the equilibrium constant.

00:36:40.298 --> 00:36:51.909
And so the ksp is the
solubility product,

00:36:51.909 --> 00:36:55.980
and it's an
equilibrium constant.

00:36:55.980 --> 00:37:11.562
Equilibrium constant that
is related to solubility.

00:37:11.562 --> 00:37:17.802
It is literally the product of
solubilities in equilibrium.

00:37:17.802 --> 00:37:19.170
That is literally what it means.

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:21.672
So we're going to do some
examples to see that.

00:37:21.672 --> 00:37:26.177
It's an equilibrium-- so it's
a constant of equilibrium.

00:37:26.177 --> 00:37:27.645
And it's called--
Gesundheit-- it's

00:37:27.645 --> 00:37:29.580
called the solubility
product, because it's

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:35.353
related to the equilibrium
of dissolving something,

00:37:35.353 --> 00:37:39.523
dissolving some
solute in a solvent.

00:37:39.523 --> 00:37:42.827
Now we're going to do
examples with salts.

00:37:42.827 --> 00:37:44.862
So we're going to be
dissolving salts and talking

00:37:44.862 --> 00:37:49.634
about equilibrium constants
in that dissolution,

00:37:49.634 --> 00:37:51.936
otherwise known as the
solubility product.

00:37:51.936 --> 00:37:54.605
It's just an
equilibrium constant.

00:37:54.605 --> 00:37:55.806
There are many.

00:37:55.806 --> 00:37:57.608
This is the one we're
going to care about,

00:37:57.608 --> 00:37:58.609
and we're going to
do it for salts.

00:37:58.609 --> 00:38:00.278
And so one of the
things you've got to--

00:38:00.278 --> 00:38:03.681
so nacl-- that was kind of easy.

00:38:03.681 --> 00:38:08.886
That became a cation and
an anion in solution.

00:38:08.886 --> 00:38:11.389
But salts can be made of
many, many different types

00:38:11.389 --> 00:38:15.359
of cations, and there's lists
that go on and on and on.

00:38:15.359 --> 00:38:18.396
Here are some, and
they get named.

00:38:18.396 --> 00:38:20.531
So here's some of the anions.

00:38:20.531 --> 00:38:24.001
Oh, chlorine-- we
just did that one.

00:38:24.001 --> 00:38:27.872
Bromine-- you can
also have a carbonate.

00:38:27.872 --> 00:38:30.374
You could have chromates,
hydroxides, oxalates,

00:38:30.374 --> 00:38:35.613
sulfates, sulfides, and the
list goes on and on and on.

00:38:35.613 --> 00:38:37.348
There are many, many of these.

00:38:37.348 --> 00:38:38.949
They don't have to
be a single atom.

00:38:38.949 --> 00:38:43.721
It can be a part of a salt
that breaks off and has

00:38:43.721 --> 00:38:44.555
a negative charge.

00:38:44.555 --> 00:38:47.758
That's an anion, and here
are some of the cations--

00:38:47.758 --> 00:38:50.828
sodium, potassium, calcium.

00:38:50.828 --> 00:38:52.163
And you know about these things.

00:38:52.163 --> 00:38:56.067
We've been talking
about these things.

00:38:56.067 --> 00:39:00.037
And you know if it's sodium,
then you know it's only going

00:39:00.037 --> 00:39:02.707
to lose-- it's only
going to be na+.

00:39:02.707 --> 00:39:07.812
It doesn't have a second
weakly-bonded electron to lose.

00:39:07.812 --> 00:39:09.780
So it's really only
going to be na+.

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:10.915
But then you get--

00:39:10.915 --> 00:39:13.751
and calcium is going
to be calcium 2+.

00:39:13.751 --> 00:39:15.386
It's got two, magnesium two.

00:39:15.386 --> 00:39:20.524
And then transition metals
are a little more complicated.

00:39:20.524 --> 00:39:22.693
They can be a little
more complicated.

00:39:22.693 --> 00:39:24.362
Iron can be 2+ or 3+.

00:39:24.362 --> 00:39:29.433
It depends, and on and
on and on for those.

00:39:29.433 --> 00:39:31.869
And so we'll do a few examples.

00:39:31.869 --> 00:39:32.870
We'll do a few examples.

00:39:35.673 --> 00:39:37.842
We'll get into the mood.

00:39:37.842 --> 00:39:40.411
And I want to do it-- first, I'm
going to do an example that's

00:39:40.411 --> 00:39:42.146
actually a classic
example, because it

00:39:42.146 --> 00:39:45.182
doesn't dissolve very well,
but it's silver chloride.

00:39:45.182 --> 00:39:47.385
Now sodium chloride is--

00:39:47.385 --> 00:39:52.823
sodium chloride goes all the way
to the point where it basically

00:39:52.823 --> 00:39:54.392
dissolves so well.

00:39:56.994 --> 00:39:58.763
So you can imagine
the equilibrium

00:39:58.763 --> 00:40:01.799
for sodium chloride
goes way to the right.

00:40:01.799 --> 00:40:04.368
But for silver chloride,
it's different.

00:40:04.368 --> 00:40:05.870
For silver chloride,
it's different.

00:40:05.870 --> 00:40:08.339
So we're going to
look at this curve.

00:40:08.339 --> 00:40:09.640
And I'll talk about this curve.

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:10.207
Don't worry.

00:40:10.207 --> 00:40:11.575
We're coming to it.

00:40:11.575 --> 00:40:12.810
We are coming to it.

00:40:12.810 --> 00:40:16.514
Let me start in
the middle again.

00:40:16.514 --> 00:40:19.216
Because what the
equilibrium constant

00:40:19.216 --> 00:40:22.286
does, given the definition
that I have just hidden,

00:40:22.286 --> 00:40:28.025
unfortunately, is it allows
us to go back and forth.

00:40:28.025 --> 00:40:30.227
If I know the
equilibrium constant,

00:40:30.227 --> 00:40:36.000
then I can get how much one
of these ions is soluble.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:39.103
I can get the maximum
solubility of it.

00:40:39.103 --> 00:40:41.372
So let's take an example.

00:40:41.372 --> 00:40:42.807
So here's a question.

00:40:42.807 --> 00:40:58.856
How much agcl dissolves
in 1 liter of water?

00:40:58.856 --> 00:41:01.025
That's such a good question.

00:41:01.025 --> 00:41:02.359
I don't know.

00:41:02.359 --> 00:41:05.629
But I do know that
the solubility product

00:41:05.629 --> 00:41:08.199
that this equilibrium
constant for sodium chloride

00:41:08.199 --> 00:41:14.038
is 1.7 times 10
to the minus 10th.

00:41:14.038 --> 00:41:19.710
Now if I write down the
dissolution reaction,

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:24.949
sodium chloride solid
is going to go to--

00:41:27.618 --> 00:41:32.590
sorry, sodium silver--
silver plus in solution.

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:36.594
So we put the aqueous plus
chlorine minus in solution.

00:41:39.763 --> 00:41:41.799
And it is going to
go back and forth.

00:41:45.803 --> 00:41:49.707
And so the ksp for this
is going to equal--

00:41:49.707 --> 00:41:52.643
I'm going to go with the
equation that I wrote down--

00:41:52.643 --> 00:41:56.547
the concentration of silver to
the 1 times the concentration

00:41:56.547 --> 00:41:58.482
of chlorine minus--
these are ions--

00:41:58.482 --> 00:42:01.719
divided by the concentration
of silver chloride solid.

00:42:06.223 --> 00:42:07.391
Now we get to another thing.

00:42:07.391 --> 00:42:11.395
Remember, I got rid of water,
because water is everywhere.

00:42:11.395 --> 00:42:12.963
It's the solvent.

00:42:12.963 --> 00:42:16.867
So you don't need to put it into
these equilibrium calculations.

00:42:16.867 --> 00:42:22.006
Well, it's the same
thing with the solid.

00:42:22.006 --> 00:42:25.209
So if I think about
this a little bit,

00:42:25.209 --> 00:42:29.780
this equilibrium is
between dissolved.

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:35.853
It's between the ions
that are dissolving.

00:42:35.853 --> 00:42:39.523
But there from this
reaction, from the way

00:42:39.523 --> 00:42:45.162
it's written there, I've got a
solid form of silver chloride.

00:42:45.162 --> 00:42:50.067
So this is not-- so this is the
concentration of solid silver

00:42:50.067 --> 00:42:52.903
chloride in the solid.

00:42:52.903 --> 00:42:54.972
This is just a constant.

00:42:54.972 --> 00:42:56.707
That's not going to change.

00:42:56.707 --> 00:42:57.841
That's not going to change.

00:42:57.841 --> 00:43:01.412
Common misunderstanding.

00:43:01.412 --> 00:43:02.279
It doesn't change.

00:43:02.279 --> 00:43:05.316
So we get rid of it, because it
gets folded into the constant.

00:43:05.316 --> 00:43:07.418
That's always the same.

00:43:07.418 --> 00:43:10.788
The concentration of
this is always the same.

00:43:10.788 --> 00:43:12.156
And so we get rid of it.

00:43:12.156 --> 00:43:18.295
So now we have the ksp is
equal to, in this case,

00:43:18.295 --> 00:43:22.266
ag+ times cl-.

00:43:22.266 --> 00:43:24.301
Talk about solubility product.

00:43:24.301 --> 00:43:26.537
Solubilities concentrate.

00:43:26.537 --> 00:43:29.373
The solubility is--
when I add this in,

00:43:29.373 --> 00:43:31.508
it reaches some
equilibrium where they're

00:43:31.508 --> 00:43:33.444
dissolving and reforming.

00:43:33.444 --> 00:43:36.547
I wanted to know at what point
that is, what concentrations

00:43:36.547 --> 00:43:37.982
is that going to be.

00:43:37.982 --> 00:43:41.385
This is them.

00:43:41.385 --> 00:43:43.454
So solubility
product-- right there.

00:43:49.426 --> 00:43:53.030
I used the definition
of equilibrium constant.

00:43:53.030 --> 00:43:56.667
And now I can use this reaction.

00:43:56.667 --> 00:44:00.804
I can use this reaction because
the stoichiometric coefficients

00:44:00.804 --> 00:44:02.406
tell me--

00:44:02.406 --> 00:44:05.075
they tell me both what
the exponents there are--

00:44:05.075 --> 00:44:09.113
1-- but they also tell me
the relative molar amounts

00:44:09.113 --> 00:44:09.980
of these things.

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:12.549
It's the same.

00:44:12.549 --> 00:44:17.521
So if x equals the
concentration of silver plus,

00:44:17.521 --> 00:44:26.964
then it must also equal the
concentration of cl minus.

00:44:26.964 --> 00:44:28.799
If you just let x equal
the concentration,

00:44:28.799 --> 00:44:30.034
that comes from the reaction.

00:44:30.034 --> 00:44:33.037
1 in front of silver,
1 in front of chlorine.

00:44:33.037 --> 00:44:35.706
And so that means now
that you can solve it

00:44:35.706 --> 00:44:46.250
because x squared is equal to
1.7 times 10 to the minus 10th,

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:56.694
and x equals 1.3 times
10 to the minus fifth, m,

00:44:56.694 --> 00:45:00.164
and that equals
these concentrations.

00:45:00.164 --> 00:45:02.032
That equals the
concentration of silver plus.

00:45:02.032 --> 00:45:04.334
So that is how much,
in equilibrium-- this

00:45:04.334 --> 00:45:05.703
is an equilibrium
constant-- that

00:45:05.703 --> 00:45:12.476
is how much you will get of
these ions dissolved in water.

00:45:12.476 --> 00:45:20.217
Now there is a nice way to
set this up that is often

00:45:20.217 --> 00:45:21.485
taught in high school.

00:45:21.485 --> 00:45:22.219
I like it.

00:45:22.219 --> 00:45:25.189
I think it's a good
way to keep track.

00:45:25.189 --> 00:45:28.292
And so if you were
wanting to keep track,

00:45:28.292 --> 00:45:33.731
and you like tables, there's
something called the ICE table.

00:45:33.731 --> 00:45:38.535
And in this case, it's a little
bit boring, but as you'll see,

00:45:38.535 --> 00:45:43.874
it can actually wind
up being pretty useful,

00:45:43.874 --> 00:45:46.176
especially when we
add other ions, which

00:45:46.176 --> 00:45:49.580
are going to do soon.

00:45:49.580 --> 00:45:54.752
So ICE is initial
change equilibrium.

00:45:54.752 --> 00:45:57.421
Initial change equilibrium.

00:45:57.421 --> 00:46:00.124
It's just a way of keeping
track of what I just did,

00:46:00.124 --> 00:46:07.698
because I started with
some amount of solid,

00:46:07.698 --> 00:46:09.399
and I had zero.

00:46:09.399 --> 00:46:10.501
I put it into water.

00:46:10.501 --> 00:46:17.875
I had zero, but then I added
1.3 times 10 to the minus fifth,

00:46:17.875 --> 00:46:22.212
and I added 1.3 times
10 to the minus fifth.

00:46:22.212 --> 00:46:28.852
And so my equilibrium is a
little less, a little less

00:46:28.852 --> 00:46:31.622
solid.

00:46:31.622 --> 00:46:33.991
And over here, it's
just these numbers.

00:46:37.728 --> 00:46:43.700
That was indeed boring,
but have no fear.

00:46:43.700 --> 00:46:46.203
It becomes helpful.

00:46:46.203 --> 00:46:48.572
It becomes helpful because
what we're going to do next--

00:46:48.572 --> 00:46:49.807
not right now--

00:46:49.807 --> 00:46:52.709
but on Wednesday,
what we're going to do

00:46:52.709 --> 00:46:56.747
is we're going to mess
with this equilibrium.

00:46:56.747 --> 00:46:59.082
Oh, equilibrium, I'm
going to throw you off.

00:46:59.082 --> 00:47:00.551
And equilibrium is
saying, no you're

00:47:00.551 --> 00:47:05.389
not, because I'm equilibrium,
and I will always be found.

00:47:05.389 --> 00:47:08.725
And that's called Le Chatelier's
Principle, and what that does

00:47:08.725 --> 00:47:10.527
is it allows us
to really go back,

00:47:10.527 --> 00:47:15.632
and we get to actually add a
bunch of chlorine ions to this,

00:47:15.632 --> 00:47:19.069
and we're going to see what
happens to this balance.

00:47:19.069 --> 00:47:21.505
It will find equilibrium.

00:47:21.505 --> 00:47:24.274
And we have the tools now
to know how to find it.

00:47:24.274 --> 00:47:26.476
That's that green curve there.

00:47:26.476 --> 00:47:28.178
This is what we're
going to work with.

00:47:28.178 --> 00:47:29.813
There's the concentration
of chlorine.

00:47:33.083 --> 00:47:35.586
There's a concentration
of silver in ions.

00:47:35.586 --> 00:47:38.355
You see the dissolved chlorine
on the x-axis, dissolved silver

00:47:38.355 --> 00:47:38.922
on the y-axis.

00:47:38.922 --> 00:47:39.923
And look at that.

00:47:39.923 --> 00:47:44.094
I've got that point
b there that is

00:47:44.094 --> 00:47:46.463
where these things
have found equilibrium

00:47:46.463 --> 00:47:48.966
when it's not messed with.

00:47:48.966 --> 00:47:52.436
Now the thing is, if you
were a reaction happening,

00:47:52.436 --> 00:47:54.705
and you hadn't gotten
to equilibrium yet--

00:47:54.705 --> 00:47:57.507
so I hadn't added
much silver chloride--

00:47:57.507 --> 00:48:01.078
if that was the case, then
you might be at point d.

00:48:01.078 --> 00:48:04.281
Notice that the reaction
quotient, q, is less

00:48:04.281 --> 00:48:07.718
than the equilibrium constant.

00:48:07.718 --> 00:48:08.719
q is less.

00:48:08.719 --> 00:48:10.420
That means it's
going to be driven

00:48:10.420 --> 00:48:13.190
to dissolve more than
it precipitates until it

00:48:13.190 --> 00:48:16.526
gets to point B on this curve.

00:48:16.526 --> 00:48:21.665
You could also be way, way out
with a much higher q point, e,

00:48:21.665 --> 00:48:24.201
and you know it has
to find equilibrium.

00:48:24.201 --> 00:48:25.802
It's going to find
equilibrium, which

00:48:25.802 --> 00:48:27.604
is what we just calculated.

00:48:27.604 --> 00:48:29.439
Those are the concentrations
of equilibrium.

00:48:29.439 --> 00:48:31.541
It has to get back to point
B. It's going to do it

00:48:31.541 --> 00:48:34.678
by precipitating out solid.

00:48:34.678 --> 00:48:35.779
It's gone too far.

00:48:38.815 --> 00:48:41.885
But you can see that
equilibrium can also be had

00:48:41.885 --> 00:48:42.886
and will be had.

00:48:42.886 --> 00:48:44.621
It shall be had.

00:48:44.621 --> 00:48:47.991
Because k never changes if
you fix the temperature.

00:48:47.991 --> 00:48:50.060
k is the same.

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:52.195
So I can mess with
this in other ways.

00:48:52.195 --> 00:48:54.498
I can go and add a
whole bunch of chlorine,

00:48:54.498 --> 00:48:57.267
and it will come back down
onto that green curve.

00:48:57.267 --> 00:48:59.169
It will come back down
onto that green curve,

00:48:59.169 --> 00:49:01.171
which is the curve
that guarantees

00:49:01.171 --> 00:49:03.140
k is the same constant.

00:49:03.140 --> 00:49:05.542
That's what that green curve is.

00:49:05.542 --> 00:49:08.545
And there's one last point
I'm going to make today,

00:49:08.545 --> 00:49:10.714
and that is units.

00:49:10.714 --> 00:49:17.020
Units of k depend--

00:49:17.020 --> 00:49:18.722
it was like the rate law.

00:49:18.722 --> 00:49:26.363
It depends on the reaction.

00:49:26.363 --> 00:49:30.767
Look, a concentration
is a concentration.

00:49:30.767 --> 00:49:32.369
That was deep.

00:49:32.369 --> 00:49:36.139
Units are capital
M moles per liter.

00:49:36.139 --> 00:49:39.209
But that means that k--

00:49:39.209 --> 00:49:42.980
k is two of those
multiplied together.

00:49:42.980 --> 00:49:50.320
So in this case, k has
units of m squared.

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:53.290
It must, or it wouldn't work.

00:49:53.290 --> 00:49:54.891
k can have units of m cubed.

00:49:57.494 --> 00:49:59.429
We will pick up
here on Wednesday,

00:49:59.429 --> 00:50:03.600
and we'll cover more
of this, more examples

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:06.503
before we move on
to acids and bases.

